    <entry xml:lang="en">
        <title>Horiz0n</title>
        <published>2026-07-23T00:00:00+00:00</published>
        <updated>2026-07-23T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
        
        <author>
          <name>
            
              Unknown
            
          </name>
        </author>
        
        <link rel="alternate" type="text/html" href="https://DreamWeave-MP.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/horiz0n/"/>
        <id>https://DreamWeave-MP.github.io/S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh/horiz0n/</id>
        
        <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;I was discussing the concept of occlusion culling with someone recently.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I told them that the engine has no defense against extremely draw-call bound areas, such as Old Ebonheart.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, I kept thinking about it, and eventually realized that was bullshit - because we DO have frustum and distance cull. These are the oldest tricks in the book for optimizing framerates in Morrowind, going all the way back to the classic &lt;code&gt;Morrowind FPS Optimizer&lt;&#x2F;code&gt; app from however long ago.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The problem that I found with prior approaches was that they were &lt;em&gt;all&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; dedicated to the idea of &lt;em&gt;saving&lt;&#x2F;em&gt; your framerate at all times. You&#x27;d get your frames back, but they wouldn&#x27;t expand your view distance back out when the headroom returned.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thus was born, Horiz0n.&lt;&#x2F;p&gt;

  
  
    
    
  
  &lt;img src=&quot;https:&#x2F;&#x2F;DreamWeave-MP.github.io&#x2F;S3ctors-S3cret-St4sh&#x2F;img&#x2F;horiz0n.png&quot; alt=&quot;Horiz0n - View Distance Manager&quot; class=&quot;center&quot; style=&quot;border-radius: 8px;&quot; decoding=&quot;async&quot; loading=&quot;lazy&quot;&#x2F;&gt;</summary>
        
    </entry>
